<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Rostock</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1419">1419</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of Rostock, founded in 1419 in the Hanseatic city of Rostock with papal approval, is one of the oldest universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the oldest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> region. In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it drew students from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and the Baltic, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> it became a stronghold of Lutheran orthodoxy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-rostock.jpg" alt="University of Rostock" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown author Vector: Lệ Xuân · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siegel_Universit%C3%A4t_Rostock_1419.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The university grew out of the prosperity that Rostock had gained as a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. Pope Martin V confirmed its foundation in 1419, making it the fifth university established in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and the oldest in continental northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> area. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> five universities are older, but only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> have operated continuously since their foundation, which makes Rostock the third oldest German university in continuous operation. Throughout the 15th and 16th centuries it had about 500 students a year and was among the most important universities in Germany and northern Europe, with many students from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and other lands around the Baltic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Political struggles and pressure from the church drove the university to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a> in 1437, where it remained until 1443, and teaching took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> from 1487 to 1488. A few years after the city, the university became Protestant in 1542, and Humanism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> became its defining characteristics. In the 16th century Rostock was the first university to teach Copernicanism, introduced by Professor Duncan Liddel. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> it played only a regional role for about two centuries. When ownership passed from the city to the state of the Grand Duchy of Mecklenburg-Schwerin in 1827, its position improved, and the second half of the 19th century saw much building and a return of its reputation.</p><p>On the 500th anniversary, on 12 November 1919, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-planck">Max Planck</a> received honorary doctorates, which made Rostock the first institution of higher learning to honour Einstein in this way. Einstein's doctorate was not revoked under Nazi rule, for reasons that remain unknown. The psychologist David Katz and the dean of the medical faculty Hans Moral were among those who lost their posts in 1933. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, in the Soviet zone, the university reopened on 24 February 1946. From 1976 until German reunification it bore the name Wilhelm-Pieck-Universität.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Many notable students and teachers have had ties with Rostock. They include the astronomer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe">Tycho Brahe</a>, who studied there in 1566, the humanist Ulrich von Hutten, who wrote his first important work there in 1509, the Swedish statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axel-oxenstierna">Axel Oxenstierna</a>, who studied from 1599 to 1601, and the archaeologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a>, who took his doctorate in 1869. Four Nobel laureates are associated with it: Albrecht Kossel, Hans Spemann, Karl von Frisch and Otto Stern, who both held professorships in the early 1920s. The university's botanical garden and a zoological collection founded in 1775 by Oluf Gerhard Tychsen are among its collections.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Rostock” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dukes shared power with strong estates of knights and towns, including the Hanseatic ports of Rostock and Wismar, and <b>Rostock University</b> was founded in 1419.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg">Duchy of Mecklenburg</a></li></ul></section>
